<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Achaemenid Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">525 BCE – 404 BCE · capital Memphis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Egypt as a satrapy of the Persian Empire, ruled by Persian kings who were also crowned as pharaohs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 525 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt">Memphis</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> counts the Persian kings as the Twenty-seventh Dynasty. The Egyptian official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udjahorresnet">Udjahorresnet</a> helped Cambyses adopt pharaonic titles.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt">Memphis</a>, collecting tribute in grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> had Egyptian laws collected, completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> canal and built the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-hibis">Hibis</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharga-oasis">Kharga Oasis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> served as the administrative language beside Egyptian, and a garrison of Jewish soldiers at Elephantine left a rich archive of Aramaic papyri. Revolts broke out in 486 BCE and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inaros-ii">Inaros</a>, who was supported by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, around 460 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-ii">Darius II</a>, the Saite noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyrtaeus">Amyrtaeus</a> rebelled in 404 BCE and freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/twenty-eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> reconquered the country in 343 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/achaemenid-egypt.jpg" alt="Achaemenid Egypt" width="320" height="324"><figcaption>Image: Mossmaps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_part_of_the_Achaemenid_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>525 BCE – 404 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Memphis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cambyses II, Darius I, Xerxes I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion, Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian, Aramaic, Old Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Twenty-seventh Dynasty, First Persian Period, Satrapy of Mudraya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-464&amp;focus=achaemenid-egypt" aria-label="Achaemenid Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5493 1088 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Achaemenid Egypt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5813 1344L6140 1251M5813 1344L5796 1305M5813 1344L5796 1305"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5796 1305h0M5796 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5813 1344h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5813 1344h0